## Investigation 1 - Successful Logon Analysis

### Event Details

| Username  | Logon Type | Date & Time              |
| --------- | ---------- | ------------------------ |
| SENTINAL$ | 5          | 31/05/2026 - 09:31:46 AM |
| SENTINAL$ | 5          | 31/05/2026 - 09:31:46 AM |
| SENTINAL$ | 5          | 31/05/2026 - 09:29:42 AM |

### Analysis

The account **SENTINAL$** was observed generating multiple successful authentication events.

The dollar sign (`$`) at the end of the account name indicates that it is likely a system-managed or service-related account.

The events used **Logon Type 5 (Service Logon)**, which is commonly associated with Windows services authenticating and starting in the background.

### Assessment

* Activity appears legitimate.
* No indicators of unauthorized access were observed.
* Multiple successful logons are consistent with normal service operations.

---

## Investigation 2 - Failed Logon Analysis

### Event Details

| Account Name | Logon Type | Failure Reason                   | Date & Time              |
| ------------ | ---------- | -------------------------------- | ------------------------ |
| SENTINAL$    | 2          | Unknown username or bad password | 31/05/2026 - 12:22:43 PM |
| SENTINAL$    | 2          | Unknown username or bad password | 31/05/2026 - 12:22:43 PM |

### Analysis

The failed logon events were intentionally generated during testing by entering incorrect credentials.

The events recorded **Logon Type 2 (Interactive Logon)**, indicating a local user authentication attempt at the system.

The failure reason reported:

> Unknown username or bad password

This behavior is expected when incorrect credentials are supplied.

### Assessment

* Activity was user-generated for testing purposes.
* No evidence of brute-force behavior.
* No malicious activity identified.

